Q: String hashing ignoring order A: <p>There's no need to sort the characters before you hash them. Usually, you're designing the hash specifically to assure that differences like this will produce different results. But it's pretty easy write a hash function that produces the same results regardless of the order of the characters in the input.</p>
<p>Here's one pretty simple example. I wrote (and tested) it in C++, but it should translate to other languages pretty easily.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int hash(std::string const &amp;s) {
    int i=0;
    for (auto ch : s)
        i += ch;
    return i;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I would note that you'd normally consider this a pretty horrible hash function--but that's unavoidable within the constraint you've given.</p>
<p>Link to complete program with test code:</p>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/8K9K1PxWT" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/8K9K1PxWT</a></p>

Q: Is my maxHeapify logic correct? I have nested the right child check within the left child check A: <p>The requirements for <code>maxHeapify</code> are not standardized<sup>1</sup>, so whether or not your logic is correct depends on your requirements. Most likely, your functional requirement is presented at the level of heap sort, something like:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If the given node is less than (at least) one of its children, swap the given node with its largest child and repeat this process for that child.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>At this level, it is not specified how to perform the check, so any correct implementation is allowed. <strong>If this matches your requirements then your implementation looks correct.</strong> Providing requirements at this level, rather than specifying each step precisely, allows for flexibility in implementation.</p>
<p>For example, if an implementation of heap sort is to be compared to other sorting algorithms (as part of a lesson on sorting), then it is beneficial to the student for the code to be clear and not require an explanation of why it is correct. Otherwise, the student might get hung up on verifying correctness and miss the comparison to other sorting strategies. As you demonstrated, nesting the conditions requires such an explanation, so this is not desirable in this context (which could explain why you've seen so many examples of non-nested checks).</p>
<p>As another example, a widely-used library does not need to cater to people reading its code, but does need to cater to a wide variety of situations.
That is, code clarity is less important than execution speed (which in turn is less important than correct functionality). In this context, every little trick to improve speed is desirable. Nesting the conditions is a step down this path.</p>
<p>So, yes, you can nest the conditions if you want to, and the result still qualifies as &quot;making a heap&quot;. Whether or not you should depends on the context. For educating others, keep the code straight-forward. For supporting others' work, go all-out on innovating optimizations. For your own work, using a trusted library might be best.</p>
<hr />
<p><sub><sup>1</sup> I have seen &quot;heapify&quot; used in some cases to refer to the question's function (enforcing the heap property at a node assuming that both child sub-trees satisfy the heap property), and in other cases for a function that creates a heap out of an array (which tends to call the question's function for each non-leaf node). The point is that there are no standard or universal requirements for a function named &quot;heapify&quot;.</sub></p>
<h2>Optimize using the contrapositive</h2>
<p><em>The contrapositive of &quot;if there is no left child then there cannot be a right child&quot; is &quot;if there is a right child then there is a left child&quot;. These are logically equivalent.</em></p>
<p>Since I mentioned widely-used libraries, I looked in one such library (an implementation of the C++ Standard Library) to see what was done there. As expected, this code is highly optimized. One interesting optimization is that instead of nesting the left- and right- child checks each time the code runs, the left-child check was taken out of the repeated code. This makes sense, since if the right child exists, there is no need to check the left. The left child needs to be checked only when there is no right child, which happens only at the end of the heap traversal.</p>
<p>Here is what this optimization would look like if applied to the question's code (with recursion replaced by a loop). This still follows the functional requirement I gave earlier, so it demonstrates how much freedom an implementation has.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>void maxHeapify(int i) { // adapted from `__adjust_heap`
    int largest = i;

    while (rightChild(largest) &lt; size())
    {
        largest = rightChild(largest);
        if (heap[largest - 1] &gt; heap[largest])
        {
            largest--; // `largest` is now the left child
        }
        if (heap[i] &gt;= heap[largest])
        {
            return;
        }
        swap(i, largest); // swap by indices
        i = largest;
    }
    // If `largest` has a left child but no right child
    if (leftChild(largest) &lt; size())
    {
        largest = leftChild(largest);
        if (heap[largest] &gt; heap[i])
        {
            swap(i, largest); // swap by indices
            // There is nothing more to do since `largest` has no children.
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
